[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a8bfec-0d80-5145-bf70-a11560b29dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305549} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac7ef6c-7eb4-5e2f-adf7-c6d5ad9f758e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305563} {:answer/id #uuid "3df59d85-639e-5277-9c54-6a5453133ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305750} {:answer/id #uuid "87e82337-fda3-5c0a-842e-943dc3fc6a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306447} {:answer/id #uuid "fe36cadf-c702-5858-b44a-dc073a8e2c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe36cadf-c702-5858-b44a-dc073a8e2c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80e6d38-96d8-59ed-a9b4-31e40da4dd1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3954ad07-64f7-58a6-b0b9-0080fcfa7904" :question/math-academy-id "q-334854" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used the fact that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1.$ Hence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, near $x=0$ we know that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≈x,$ so $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ behaves like $\\frac{1}{x},$ which becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c784766-558f-50d3-9a57-f232cf17020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305729} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d996ff-d97e-5b64-b844-757ddc94fdd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306838} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fff127-b6e3-51cb-8c39-659f91472ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306877} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d04a5e-33cd-5aa7-a4cc-5d2a8bc34b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307417} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0171e5-e531-581c-bd67-2e1021c385f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8fff127-b6e3-51cb-8c39-659f91472ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306877} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d67219-5e4b-5e06-b98c-3df044a80ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3acb3544-bfaf-54f0-ae53-b0963dc83190" :question/math-academy-id "q-334861" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03356d-c8ee-50a1-89d5-5d2d4aca6a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305437} {:answer/id #uuid "47be19a1-dcff-542a-b5e6-7a82c6ae7abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305848} {:answer/id #uuid "83365ba2-9f57-5fcc-ae2d-8da7f8ed611a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306404} {:answer/id #uuid "92e7aa34-6bc4-5f38-8fb0-4743707c007d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306542} {:answer/id #uuid "c83c2552-815a-5e4c-8646-e1fb1d7d58c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e03356d-c8ee-50a1-89d5-5d2d4aca6a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305437} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b58e4e3-d62f-57b6-b69e-ce4260120c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c936d65e-2929-5c4f-9009-0c5a872435df" :question/math-academy-id "q-334862" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e95dcb1-6590-50e8-9d9d-404c1fadbb4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305441} {:answer/id #uuid "36cca004-3d86-5d1e-abbd-fc750ff9bcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305680} {:answer/id #uuid "618d5cf5-94ba-5d7e-950f-f0546dc4c910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306094} {:answer/id #uuid "8116eedc-ae1c-5d3d-816a-6751880ac9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306382} {:answer/id #uuid "b1975609-17be-5396-9d96-8b263bd48139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1975609-17be-5396-9d96-8b263bd48139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306818} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ade12d2-f3a1-5ea6-91ff-166134ba534a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97b1feee-658e-5bb1-8630-22a1f9c057aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-334867" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d786ad4-e503-5ef7-a81c-9919dddad716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306199} {:answer/id #uuid "7110bea9-89dc-5ce2-97e2-8f63d208864a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306230} {:answer/id #uuid "818c85f1-6793-564b-8adc-6716cea85414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306390} {:answer/id #uuid "adb06e6a-ad6b-58e6-ad76-b455ba490a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306789} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d25232-13dc-5820-9097-b4fba2cccfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d25232-13dc-5820-9097-b4fba2cccfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307440} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac18b4c-ee9e-5737-bdae-19eb2d9721d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a21bb853-c833-53c0-be43-6d5a120a6d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-334870" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc1324e-690e-5186-b0b0-4fc893b3d2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305461} {:answer/id #uuid "326d39f5-809f-54c0-ad59-3063be64072a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305625} {:answer/id #uuid "bde69883-7f44-504e-be2d-e3c6c7eb3462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306923} {:answer/id #uuid "df588f2d-686f-56a7-bb62-086f968c7602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307220} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a0ada8-a30e-558e-8e5a-7f5212ad4f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "326d39f5-809f-54c0-ad59-3063be64072a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305625} :answer-field/id #uuid "abfd1a4e-c2d4-53b8-a2a9-b49672cd8880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "448418f7-1bbb-5414-b0a2-b045e5f85194" :question/math-academy-id "q-334881" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise continuous function on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295c2af6-381d-5dc0-89a3-f44dd6551914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305539} {:answer/id #uuid "2abe050f-2c09-5467-94fe-83ab3e54d011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305561} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccaa3ba-d0c4-5f16-ab4f-e1ffa942193b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305733} {:answer/id #uuid "c121bfcf-3092-56bd-861d-4ae353f48868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306952} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2124a3-8508-5df7-8512-59cb7b38e665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2abe050f-2c09-5467-94fe-83ab3e54d011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305561} :answer-field/id #uuid "80338bfe-b2ca-534f-b618-face87b04079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28363017-b95e-52d8-9173-1894ab5a3d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-335019" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b276fe-3cf7-57cf-b9f4-d196045a2203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305239} {:answer/id #uuid "15292b7f-56e6-5bd3-95b8-c9e0c4571d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305334} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7a331b-5455-5917-b9da-a066f46db285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306646} {:answer/id #uuid "e954cb4c-cec6-5790-b690-df3555baaa85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307302} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2ba17-dd4d-5fce-8f72-70ec76aaaf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9d2ba17-dd4d-5fce-8f72-70ec76aaaf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307466} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf558613-5788-5902-8832-fe0df25b00a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e125563-3523-5218-87f9-08cecc055fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335020" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c8a06b-1e07-5bf2-983f-fbe9e1c056eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306085} {:answer/id #uuid "afbf8e20-88cf-5957-9cbb-a0bc767d4499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306807} {:answer/id #uuid "bdacb380-cee2-55f7-919b-f028da0a7a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306921} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6446d8-deb8-5672-8df1-aaf4d20ff6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307066} {:answer/id #uuid "fadece3a-f6fa-56d7-8f17-29942f61dae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fadece3a-f6fa-56d7-8f17-29942f61dae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307475} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee92aff-b361-5f51-aa51-85a79dcc012c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38e1f503-d528-5681-bd45-38417a3415be" :question/math-academy-id "q-335048" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489cf97e-6c0f-5597-800e-5df4c84dc7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309302} {:answer/id #uuid "52f463cd-7b7b-5861-a0e6-e446661d097c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e89d5e4-0385-57c3-bb82-acd44dba8514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309455} {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7cc0b7-396a-5b5c-b805-605879748b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073} :answer-field/id #uuid "27253de7-dbc7-5838-a4cb-f5a95a31f6a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8a1841-dc47-5a84-bd2d-e5c201943d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335246" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1674bcea-39fc-5aad-aca7-372a177fe050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308917} {:answer/id #uuid "25e64bcc-0147-5181-9ef9-b6cb514ee5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309029} {:answer/id #uuid "511cfe36-d25a-5e4f-af0d-d2735ed975a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309361} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5d3830-b200-5801-9be5-e52c9b90e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309864} {:answer/id #uuid "955b3dd8-2188-5b71-8408-64ec11de032b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5d3830-b200-5801-9be5-e52c9b90e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309864} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85287b0-57f7-5ef2-b815-ce3761ebab78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c23f2cc-82f0-5867-b00d-89c6883bf817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308834} {:answer/id #uuid "22fe8dc0-6cee-58c2-8654-f942a7436068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309011} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb0f1cb-d3fe-542e-9b7a-c1e573068d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310029} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b7bfc2-5415-538d-a056-230b0f1b053d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310176} {:answer/id #uuid "dfedda34-e72c-5a4e-b2ae-71901b0f875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22fe8dc0-6cee-58c2-8654-f942a7436068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309011} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d724d1-f317-5650-bac8-04e43dc1b9b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57d1f543-d099-580f-9280-d60da64177c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f1d1d5-26f2-5996-9b45-e843073eebd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308784} {:answer/id #uuid "105ce355-6bc0-5eac-ab1d-30bf73c3e3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "1489c33b-6f54-52d8-aae5-e12710732f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "8dad0b51-3e0e-5303-9848-e926f7285ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309871} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8570d1-eab0-562a-97e3-ada215b2c56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f1d1d5-26f2-5996-9b45-e843073eebd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308784} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cc132b-f46c-51a6-9635-8bd3d3a39e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7963456-3a78-5141-94e0-577dece1c8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f46424-29be-5d3d-b031-687406eee03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308879} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ed01f6-8a08-5ec8-861d-b3e1e088b9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310063} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c13585-d0f3-59b9-8c0d-4e5fcb99b25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310243} {:answer/id #uuid "c5889f99-7390-5c8b-ace3-4be3fb47757b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310334} {:answer/id #uuid "d640ac00-bf89-544e-bb7c-ad79cfdd36ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8c13585-d0f3-59b9-8c0d-4e5fcb99b25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310243} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e9c6d7-afe3-5aee-99f7-a8d468714a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8de3c48f-01de-56bf-9ee6-1a3bd0e95cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335251" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (-1,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f80e846-ac27-534a-a725-f39ca5ca5e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309352} {:answer/id #uuid "9dea671c-d517-542c-9844-cfaeaa711a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310022} {:answer/id #uuid "a506c6b6-2284-564c-80c1-f230b001d945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310079} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a648f9-d339-5127-b434-7fbefbc0e4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310226} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7776d4-1560-53ec-9fcc-60878feed89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a648f9-d339-5127-b434-7fbefbc0e4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310226} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61165cc-0943-5bff-9268-8b0e0e9c0962" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b175da7-6b72-569e-916d-da964786e33a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f99b46f-1bfb-592a-8590-5899bae0952b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308858} {:answer/id #uuid "12a31e39-e59e-5a7c-af39-50efb6880801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308883} {:answer/id #uuid "77c96f06-3b49-50a4-9066-9d569fe5b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309687} {:answer/id #uuid "7c55a2a5-0062-5bfa-8d99-7f341c26802d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309719} {:answer/id #uuid "c55be731-7911-59e5-9425-6aa78a0dc81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c55be731-7911-59e5-9425-6aa78a0dc81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310332} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2593ec9-6ec0-52ae-8ade-9433bf52750e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0283e52-8fee-5c3a-a13d-c279ee722034" :question/math-academy-id "q-335253" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2437fa4d-29a9-5143-ad35-cab5794108ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309020} {:answer/id #uuid "2f79000b-1a1d-53e2-8c95-11ca44047cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309109} {:answer/id #uuid "6f88e01b-9a1f-5545-9075-0ba03094539e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9b6164-6b6f-59c4-8f76-6a4bb925415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310399} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1448e-d841-5762-bd59-43fbc9c7dd6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd9b6164-6b6f-59c4-8f76-6a4bb925415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310399} :answer-field/id #uuid "814cdb8b-e518-56eb-baee-ee5982de94e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757f1645-7b5c-5866-81f6-7ae1eefa124d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "689a4721-d905-5dd0-ab19-a3b17d5aad7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309553} {:answer/id #uuid "6f758fac-f361-5da4-8ffd-b008a2a5efe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309609} {:answer/id #uuid "76301b92-8e49-5056-8988-b54e793563f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309672} {:answer/id #uuid "e575ecf1-1749-5d8d-8a11-da063e9249a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310577} {:answer/id #uuid "f87b66e2-96d0-5b1c-a973-1e763283b85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689a4721-d905-5dd0-ab19-a3b17d5aad7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309553} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf334cf0-1f36-5980-8df6-c5f2211d44fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7fa63bd-d15a-5bf7-b7e2-ddd1185810da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3ca35c-5986-512a-a939-03679884558b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308844} {:answer/id #uuid "323851ee-65ca-58d4-aba6-3dbe85562ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309127} {:answer/id #uuid "32588f4d-1732-5291-bdb9-37cdfe9b02e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309130} {:answer/id #uuid "cc87a612-feb9-5caa-b1a0-d4d7393c6cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310388} {:answer/id #uuid "f1105306-5f44-5be5-ad98-9fbf2cd26c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1105306-5f44-5be5-ad98-9fbf2cd26c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310670} :answer-field/id #uuid "35f1abb1-a448-528a-83a3-13ad2bb497f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e65967cd-c336-53ba-a105-1c4f34ff015c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335824" :question/problem "![](images/96/96df34b3f844ee5abace182769011a3b4b2e413100fbca4cf25e829ca67948cc.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6)$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=2,$ $y=4$ and $y=6.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-2)(y-4)(y-6),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=2,4,6.$\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$ This does not correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y-4)(y-6),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=2,4,6.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,6)$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (4,6),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,4)\\cup (6,\\infty ).$\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a50a8b-6b4b-5499-bfaf-f73e029fb3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308944} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab04844-b9a7-5018-8552-6bfcb710cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309066} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd94a-3f96-5fb9-a8c4-7d16a08c5729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309595} {:answer/id #uuid "979282b7-3740-556d-8772-3318b6c3422a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309965} {:answer/id #uuid "d3931537-1e8d-5f73-8afd-1ba1b3628fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3931537-1e8d-5f73-8afd-1ba1b3628fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310451} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5b37a6-7401-5bf7-a3a8-466d6f9e917c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "870fa3ac-a172-57d1-b18b-7584f7771a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-337223" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197585d7-48ec-5bfa-bb26-ca9d9544d47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308951} {:answer/id #uuid "2af4a78e-e83e-588e-a29b-1ce8712f1067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309069} {:answer/id #uuid "5280920d-a88f-5d86-b456-83315d137ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309373} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8e9d50-9bad-5af4-b248-3eae428838e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309602} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee70eae-18ae-5be0-af64-a0fc60ceabec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197585d7-48ec-5bfa-bb26-ca9d9544d47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308951} :answer-field/id #uuid "2925ca55-ddf8-5e7e-a92f-f37ffb46b6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76e64e6f-7a8a-5b8b-bac6-612af4c245fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-337226" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{2}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03795dba-e4b3-563c-9505-932103c7f09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308771} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddef58e-faa9-5b35-abe1-0c78d4dcdfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308841} {:answer/id #uuid "438c4fcc-3c33-5727-8c46-cc5eb658abe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309259} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbf7a4-665b-5b8e-a13e-bb7aebcef7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310566} {:answer/id #uuid "f9975ab7-4dcb-58f1-ad62-e809069b4f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9975ab7-4dcb-58f1-ad62-e809069b4f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310739} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d3e201-d9fd-5f9a-ab64-8446c71c235e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b52c57b-852c-58e2-ae93-cc78b2af7920" :question/math-academy-id "q-337229" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140fb66d-7dba-5266-9485-d536777408b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308894} {:answer/id #uuid "46aca1bc-24cc-5256-bd7c-120ad086c2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309287} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d08bf-5824-5597-94aa-977ff6f39c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309319} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1ff627-9c16-5002-88d9-5ccbe9ae798f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310158} {:answer/id #uuid "dec41f0a-e626-59e6-8bc5-6c658f741ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1d08bf-5824-5597-94aa-977ff6f39c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309319} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc6f94b-06f0-5a01-a6a9-2c2acc2d8019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af31d734-fa0c-53ba-89f5-33969f158e1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337233" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}$\n- $y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042ed97e-d5ea-5837-9be9-b68218769512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308775} {:answer/id #uuid "194a19e8-ed20-5efa-9322-495da5103502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308949} {:answer/id #uuid "3021bce8-c9fb-5fd5-b406-379213be3b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309113} {:answer/id #uuid "9138feeb-64d5-5918-a962-3b7647e57e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309910} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5477dc-2f71-5c29-8219-68406c7e07c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "042ed97e-d5ea-5837-9be9-b68218769512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308775} :answer-field/id #uuid "b838dbea-d2c1-50d5-976c-d7243d3f2e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a1da43a-e002-579e-9946-31a6ae805b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-337245" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+({y}^{′})^{2}$\n- $y-x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}+\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}}=0$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21da9bb8-c049-5cb4-b242-7fc53d58be9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309002} {:answer/id #uuid "28135ff8-1d3c-5e20-8b2e-db510d594cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309050} {:answer/id #uuid "8befa612-e0bf-5637-8864-2a59a933e33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309852} {:answer/id #uuid "c37f5f21-7e6b-5baf-b911-1b95958af413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310314} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8ad1a6-dc6e-51a7-adb5-61e0148c87e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28135ff8-1d3c-5e20-8b2e-db510d594cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309050} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e64c49-7e98-5327-9e8e-29def6193e77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4fa353d-a0fb-5d32-a867-d6df2715a793" :question/math-academy-id "q-337765" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4231943f-e21b-50ba-b62b-35503d9e1c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309249} {:answer/id #uuid "762dbd80-e8cb-56f2-904c-d215284cfc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309671} {:answer/id #uuid "a68ccd64-f0de-545e-8960-d6ea998f5120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310091} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8d38b2-de59-57b9-aa32-220c842de69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310523} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f32e63-9cbc-50c5-83b3-387841bd1945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f32e63-9cbc-50c5-83b3-387841bd1945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310687} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9f0052c-6498-5ac3-bb74-14bd0b20b5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ea3d35e-7e2f-5e81-99fa-fd738c89805d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337773" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{x}y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23747e65-928b-5e4b-922e-e73b4e74a8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309017} {:answer/id #uuid "41869df2-afa5-5cf9-9e61-0be681edd492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309247} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc32ae-f43a-591b-bcf0-7e39e3a79e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310244} {:answer/id #uuid "d96b2d30-06cd-5824-8b85-a6c3aec875a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310497} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fbed37-7e33-533f-9780-dff263a9ecd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96b2d30-06cd-5824-8b85-a6c3aec875a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310497} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04e99c3-eacd-51df-af42-343b6b1f4b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7fcf4e-fdda-501c-b765-bd8e87f5aae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337774" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dfad69d-c539-5aa8-a20b-cc48752c73d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309340} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6d65b4-f9e8-5193-9ddf-a4b88db71dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309454} {:answer/id #uuid "6e68bff1-c792-51cd-80a6-4308ab9f4ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309599} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf55dfd-8ad3-5325-bea5-c4b38e781953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309853} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1d35bc-9eb2-5186-8a5b-702c7a52fc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb1d35bc-9eb2-5186-8a5b-702c7a52fc33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310750} :answer-field/id #uuid "af547b4f-a6df-56ac-aa0e-831ceeb8578c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d8bb1c-4d46-5c7b-bec5-01c857be1d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-337777" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $xy,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d87b94e-2a58-5a91-b413-e2ace8c0acef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308839} {:answer/id #uuid "3977be65-6745-5b00-9e8b-2471e205c872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309184} {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} {:answer/id #uuid "7f143cd7-e11a-59b4-bd54-f611d460cc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309741} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ed839d-2200-51a0-8970-9289668f781e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309500} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af460f7-9e3d-5968-8b8f-b6d594daad31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a6ed137-ea67-591f-bf1a-a0d1b0037ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337778" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a0694d-5ba0-59c1-a8e5-2c4573d2c912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308875} {:answer/id #uuid "24881b5f-7168-5dde-aa2f-79d5822c355c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309021} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec1c8a-0464-50bf-880d-57363f13b364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309395} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc67f7e-ec91-51b5-99c7-c36fc0d9ae01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309469} {:answer/id #uuid "ad64757b-2e99-59cc-8fd4-49759964480f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc67f7e-ec91-51b5-99c7-c36fc0d9ae01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309469} :answer-field/id #uuid "839362f3-a078-55ac-808a-c2140ece70d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5914278-adab-5478-b262-9e2341a90a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338102" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a7940b-0f3f-508b-a871-15800c6fb8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308818} {:answer/id #uuid "36582925-902c-57ac-a95b-7adf28f5b5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309163} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fd2fea-f0c6-5fc6-b76e-5c25cb8f9b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310213} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8fdc15-819c-5879-ae73-8350d36043df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310651} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf1f99a-680a-5646-b2a0-1bc10f15965f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf1f99a-680a-5646-b2a0-1bc10f15965f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310781} :answer-field/id #uuid "698a23fe-8a46-5cb4-b72e-ffe832d89c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1c5937c-0d4d-5848-8380-65f08dc10e61" :question/math-academy-id "q-338104" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1625d655-f927-5e87-b592-3baaa9fdb126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308913} {:answer/id #uuid "29a8dacf-75fd-54aa-b558-ecbcf65b465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309061} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5630f9-e515-5b3b-b57b-53d23fecbca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310018} {:answer/id #uuid "d331a172-b010-5c90-9997-3b36b66f260c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310446} {:answer/id #uuid "fd00e46c-1199-5178-b227-f274b3067b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a8dacf-75fd-54aa-b558-ecbcf65b465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b085c7-d2e7-5720-9559-563297f4198b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09178b04-bd98-588e-bede-8ee7e96e2b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312041} {:answer/id #uuid "37c76a34-ae9b-5e38-97da-1b66a8487677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312425} {:answer/id #uuid "74b0f70f-341f-56cb-ac17-2e8b98cdbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312905} {:answer/id #uuid "cc62253a-567e-5b64-a9ab-f6d7d1c562d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313573} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d27d08-7b11-540f-86bb-babed660a878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d27d08-7b11-540f-86bb-babed660a878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313956} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1db2b22-e47d-53ca-9e53-25c549e46a18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dabe6837-4031-5ea3-85c8-7a53d65944dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-338531" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1$\n- ${y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07efab25-6886-5e52-8d46-c2a559ac1019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312031} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7098cf-b116-5904-9694-dedeb9dc5128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312320} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a6a02-d4d5-5c01-b6fd-2ff5cda3a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313437} {:answer/id #uuid "ceee585a-3847-56cc-a47b-dca9a37e5e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313593} {:answer/id #uuid "d756000d-75d3-53ac-a8fe-9bffae524109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a6a02-d4d5-5c01-b6fd-2ff5cda3a0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313437} :answer-field/id #uuid "752d6c27-fdee-5fe5-8c2a-6e555a305157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce074393-6c5e-5e9b-ab9a-92fd971d7d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be47449-5ee2-57a9-8729-139c804b67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312062} {:answer/id #uuid "20b7057f-8151-5aee-b7e3-21eb04501cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312224} {:answer/id #uuid "35454e6e-1293-518d-83d7-8733d296c529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312404} {:answer/id #uuid "83a920aa-cfc7-5778-8108-3a459f40e158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313035} {:answer/id #uuid "e91f4b05-eb68-5bca-8eb6-de17a14f1767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a920aa-cfc7-5778-8108-3a459f40e158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313035} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b10264-2c46-5bec-8c28-bce288ba276a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fcfd17f-1887-5fc2-a463-d8fe70a76b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338539" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(4)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(-2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-2$). This is an **initial value problem.**"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130c81ac-e871-55cc-95b8-7a7a071fc1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312110} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6425e3-1e59-5813-a34b-10a58f5f7d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312449} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3efadc-48f9-5604-a7a8-8caf593805bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312952} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7ded43-bef8-521b-8b55-f75b5437f787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312962} {:answer/id #uuid "bc693c5e-f884-5c43-b661-2e0cd8f961c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a6425e3-1e59-5813-a34b-10a58f5f7d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312449} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f41a75e-38f6-5ddd-9832-01919cd770a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4fd4473-8c46-5808-aedb-c06540dd956d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338595" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- $3{y}^{″}-y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=4$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{2},\\,y(-1)=2,\\,y(1)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232e1687-2b34-5620-82b4-0d138f862182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312236} {:answer/id #uuid "4b38d8ec-d531-5cf9-b4c3-9bd0e7ce8dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312585} {:answer/id #uuid "735e065c-1988-56ad-8759-0134dabdff16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312888} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b8076f-3089-5a15-89d0-1919cd69c1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313484} {:answer/id #uuid "e878fa27-db60-57da-aa04-d934c1901f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232e1687-2b34-5620-82b4-0d138f862182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312236} :answer-field/id #uuid "02efa76e-cd06-50a0-bb62-a3fd2816554d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baccee23-0e1a-561a-a3ca-cf008d4fd658" :question/math-academy-id "q-339253" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=4x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+9y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=-2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c114e9e-4a35-5945-be53-ea661282072d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312821} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8738e-d541-52e5-8498-0cf69b712026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312862} {:answer/id #uuid "a838513d-8c93-5cae-a2db-0967ee3fec2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313293} {:answer/id #uuid "efbf4849-338d-5e69-8c8c-a8000a2b3187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313894} {:answer/id #uuid "f2353756-54d9-5b2e-88d7-60288c65a536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2353756-54d9-5b2e-88d7-60288c65a536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313917} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a9980f7-9c44-52eb-95f1-350e7a4948cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d74bf8-bcac-5e65-b877-61e885b1b024" :question/math-academy-id "q-339254" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y=8$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem.  It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5385c7ea-97b0-5cd3-b407-e0b5a65933bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312653} {:answer/id #uuid "60093836-8629-59c4-80d3-2bc1f040c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312745} {:answer/id #uuid "7f10b445-8e28-57fa-9ef4-9ffb5015d937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312997} {:answer/id #uuid "817a6641-15a5-5723-b261-fdac7757ebb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313019} {:answer/id #uuid "bd83da3b-2e54-5b82-960c-036904d3cfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5385c7ea-97b0-5cd3-b407-e0b5a65933bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312653} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad326b15-681f-596d-b066-c89a18877887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27876a51-c9f8-5f4d-9c9b-be85b540294f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339539" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b98914cb-c5a5-53a9-b026-3e2bf2976a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313418} {:answer/id #uuid "c7813db9-4ef5-5b5f-8d21-f012fa56d240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdd177c-9b09-58cd-9378-0fef1fe8aa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313705} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0514a8-4aa2-54dd-8f61-2feccf10f107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313845} {:answer/id #uuid "f9390918-851c-5846-8e6d-631d52dd4ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb0514a8-4aa2-54dd-8f61-2feccf10f107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313845} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c14c918-1ed3-5833-b916-578a7a9df365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "686f5a84-c1ca-551b-b5e9-fd8d6a7feafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339540" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t), &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\,t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}), &  & b(t)={13}^{2}, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=9, &  & d(t)=-\\pi , &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053f3697-b8c6-5f4c-bddf-90e6089b5f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312009} {:answer/id #uuid "14efacbd-4a68-5f00-b65f-c48221075050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312122} {:answer/id #uuid "8608580c-b3af-534d-9668-e2ae2c103214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313050} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6aa881-f44f-5466-9462-3fbc1987f153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313219} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8e6aaf-ceae-5f11-b643-0c2063cd8518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6aa881-f44f-5466-9462-3fbc1987f153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313219} :answer-field/id #uuid "a862c719-4ccd-5ed8-9143-c972a25dc1af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4edb4632-82c5-5949-9b3d-34461c2dad7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339543" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42eb79ee-12fc-5ebe-ae84-5140c7e8f9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312508} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed0038-5857-5699-b170-89ae78c852e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312733} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca7dfa-d660-570a-8248-4046d8e69f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "d9682625-a9ab-5c8a-a507-8e4b8411a9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313685} {:answer/id #uuid "ee323000-79c5-521c-98bd-93aa3131775f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca7dfa-d660-570a-8248-4046d8e69f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} :answer-field/id #uuid "997d6138-d847-5aa5-a81c-5fbc072a03ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60e129ea-a029-5585-b3d8-45191fe84a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-339547" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=7, &  & b(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385ba27a-e049-525b-a5d8-dc375ac40522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312