e30a6-55cc-547c-89a4-36a7d494b589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313267} {:answer/id #uuid "c413c015-fa40-508d-b83f-c99fd7911d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313516} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d82aed-e0de-54d4-8293-afeff2b44f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313913} {:answer/id #uuid "fc72c4d3-bc8a-5f0d-be5d-7ac8569ecce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc72c4d3-bc8a-5f0d-be5d-7ac8569ecce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313986} :answer-field/id #uuid "e809bc77-ea6a-564a-bba8-ac1ad676ebbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661fb44f-ce59-5065-83c7-98cb9e445e73" :question/math-academy-id "q-339711" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7ddb28ee5184cc0cc6dc0b7253d3dd3ed24481c8433e5937532345adb4162e3.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a node." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f82ff6ec76ab3cf26d2da516b6bf5c5d9773824043a38a26c8db8a16150bfc86.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5f6705-4881-5650-92af-e3b138d4fe86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312067} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3e9edc-fadd-51ea-a978-e8c9ae66d8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312311} {:answer/id #uuid "7700cff6-472c-5f80-894a-ab455fc8734b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312926} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4020fc-cf71-50b0-a3af-47dfc3a110ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313715} {:answer/id #uuid "e6667333-19ab-53d3-96be-160f590f4806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b3e9edc-fadd-51ea-a978-e8c9ae66d8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9402f21-9b36-5e88-9011-858533a92c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68a9f1f0-e085-5ed7-bf4c-69fbdf0774fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340510" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3)$\n- $f(x+1)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3c9ec9-3e3f-5a51-83a1-ecaf5ba59a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312051} {:answer/id #uuid "2de2ae84-9a3f-5b2f-a1d8-cf9e74575120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312336} {:answer/id #uuid "3b052727-de15-5715-a293-8c230c5f3534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312453} {:answer/id #uuid "8c33f0b8-66ab-5e6b-ae1d-5f21a75d6b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313096} {:answer/id #uuid "91efda95-03ba-5c22-8779-0d55badcc6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a3c9ec9-3e3f-5a51-83a1-ecaf5ba59a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312051} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5798c91-b3ce-5022-a717-9dc43c87d686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea143c38-1de5-5608-b272-c1150223e597" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08181d23-79ae-56bf-b3d7-d896e16614e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312033} {:answer/id #uuid "0e41219f-33fc-5669-a140-e4fc660ecc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312078} {:answer/id #uuid "91e9b2da-61b0-5875-9626-b1a36474c2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313143} {:answer/id #uuid "99b360f2-bcd6-59e6-ba06-ec77acde7be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313193} {:answer/id #uuid "bebb78e0-74b5-5ec3-ac2a-38d2b8e19cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e9b2da-61b0-5875-9626-b1a36474c2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313143} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e11b556-cf2e-5aa1-8ec2-9fa20deecf15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47637532-5815-59ac-9760-d5eb87953b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341237" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+4\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e82cbb5-72e9-5b8b-95fb-01801ff4b6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315650} {:answer/id #uuid "877cb6e8-1876-5f80-a0b2-98a2462c4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315878} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e12c5-211f-5ac8-b470-1cfd82428117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd5717-22ff-5564-89cb-a84b93a092d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-7116bb6897fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315377} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ac1933-5ac4-59df-9437-0db31d242812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "278579d2-6c97-5a07-b8af-1fbac0d66174" :question/math-academy-id "q-34174" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b7d38b-59e8-5c99-baf1-8ebca2bb3e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315394} {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315417} {:answer/id #uuid "36e2aefa-28e8-537d-a0b0-a16e4b71dc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315437} {:answer/id #uuid "697157b6-d0b7-514a-85b2-51f3d2ff6cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315714} {:answer/id #uuid "f5260582-e5a1-582d-8cf8-b708b93e1374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33432922-1ca0-5ab4-81d5-8b2d3857e426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315417} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe195ec3-885a-500a-a80c-d7dfafea1cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ba2fd2-65b4-550d-8354-517b0631809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342275" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d51af1-e503-5a7e-b5c4-3ae217bde11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315305} {:answer/id #uuid "24800d2a-5fc1-582a-9fd9-908f2235d74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315323} {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315401} {:answer/id #uuid "a902dbd9-63b6-5ef6-bbe8-ce3be56fbbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316067} {:answer/id #uuid "ac091dc7-3951-5fcc-8f2c-d6262ff6a701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317ac0b1-2810-51b7-8d45-3ccec2ac879c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315401} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9a3fe6-a8ea-5181-ac25-7f8ac8fec6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "905b994c-71d1-59e7-beab-bb9f96b34458" :question/math-academy-id "q-342279" :question/problem "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325de50a-bd2e-5b08-8def-2a320b8858b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315411} {:answer/id #uuid "43571603-dcf9-5d5f-b4d5-068e7b710ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315507} {:answer/id #uuid "4960eaa9-2ef2-55f9-9e74-72b970724f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315540} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca10b7-c88f-59b6-99d2-5015abb15acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316370} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fc1a8c-df7b-5734-81b5-5015a8867753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218631648