n "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cb6212-1c96-5038-9cf1-1238bb9a8ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315352} {:answer/id #uuid "57690396-7e42-5f88-8067-3e0a26680548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315625} {:answer/id #uuid "82fb4501-cf3a-522e-827f-c28d838cb604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315842} {:answer/id #uuid "a2429bd6-3fbb-5688-9cac-94527928908e" :answer/t