ro. So, $x=-2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=1,$ since both denominators at $x=1$ equal zero. So, $x=1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae40602-954a-5af2-a16f-70c8ad4d9d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315369} {:answer/id #uuid "65e144ee-8cc6-5627-9fd5-800e85b51ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315684} {:answer/id #uuid "a2df0e86-4fc7-5151-9b72-843102b86f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316038} {:answer/id #uuid "a578df17-e347-574e-adf2-dc8917486d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052} {:answer/id #uuid "fa833f8f-0902-57f8-a2ef-6721ba7656f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a578df17-e347-574e-adf2-dc8917486d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052} :answer-field/id #uuid "b890767c-b1ed-5f63-bdea-2b15a7a09aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac58339-a2d8-5129-8e48-737c1bfcbe88" :question/math-academy-id "q-342299" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1e8f46-60a5-5fb3-9850-82cb6f2d4656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/t