 and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6bfc6c-a7e5-5e4c-9b1c-26531be23577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315212} {:answer/id #uuid "18b788e9-ced1-5047-a93b-4f6ef4d77290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315262} {:answer/id #uuid "43ab01a2-9423-58fc-84e4-413182dc77d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315512} {:answer/id #uuid "da4ce323-39e7-5f2b-b8e3-4467482f2a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316336} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5d86ae-a132-5fdd-8166-0d8caff84d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb5d86ae-a132-5fdd-8166-0d8caff84d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316425} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a6cb7c-2df0-5221-a66b-d3d689406e86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38f95697-88a9-5ddb-b3e7-2448323c712f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342306" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The functions $(x-2)P(x)$ and $(x-2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The point $x=2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{5x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{(x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=2$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d86984a-d0b4-59d6-bae0-cc1bfdb6ab95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315383} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbf8515-1858-5691-a28a-c14211d8a5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315656} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd29879-0271-5d20-b517-c57a7ad349a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315914} {:answer/id #uuid "9ade6c73-ee72-50b3-b392-14fc93d501c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315983} {:answer/id #uuid "ca29fd0a-80a1-52b0-b29a-2c8cea00c14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d86984a-d0b4-59d6-bae0-cc1bfdb6ab95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315383} :answer-field/id #uuid "230e719c-ae2d-5d69-acfb-b8ee92f05ef7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8575f381-396c-5d27-97ba-96e6bcb9f145" :question/math-academy-id "q-342307" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11612c35-89b8-544d-a490-1e8b3ee9ccd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None