{y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d089498-ff23-5988-980b-fdcba437a889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317613} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1a4f35-4bc7-54f3-ab98-800c3d8073a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317655} {:answer/id #uuid "429ee94c-2031-5661-ad02-e80e3a408d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317782} {:answer/id #uuid "9fea6759-8d33-5660-a994-9f3591752561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318111} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d6a4dd-4222-5ace-b345-e51600226db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429ee94c-2031-5661-ad02-e80e3a408d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317782} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a07640-1e84-5075-9bf7-71dd87597ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c6e61ad-5f9a-5a1b-a420-c0ce7a3cc35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34700" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement II is false, while statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198edc06-3968-55e9-a166-79faa35ca2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317649} {:answer/id #uuid "21144eeb-acb9-570b-9b58-615c44dd1df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317668} {:answer/id #uuid "ad12cc37-4902-5bd6-9dcb-f71cdb5196a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318153} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbffdeb-38f6-5247-83dc-27e3b1a3f32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318254} {:answer/id #uuid "dc287b40-ce00-5b56-8494-e4cbaeea62ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc287b40-ce00-5b56-8494-e4cbaeea62ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f78e3b07-4c12-5122-8928-29065e15e880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1abddd4-93e9-501d-bf47-b8161bdb88cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-34714" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38075968-acaa-5735-8250-410ae1fdd456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317745} {:answer/id #uuid "4a51baa5-9f95-53e0-a291-7410f106f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317809} {:answer/id #uuid "51019c31-09c7-5ff0-80b7-1c36b4b14a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317832} {:answer/id #uuid "950cacd2-b831-5b9b-8837-9cf034672b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318075} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d2ffa4-2f30-5bdc-9dd6-f5a911dc04cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "950cacd2-b831-5b9b-8837-9cf034672b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318075} :answer-field/id #uuid "d876802a-eaf2-5d52-b81f-b982c93b64b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fa1c1b6-aa0b-5eb1-b0a4-3972a58dc98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34768" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{3},{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{3},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{3},{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${b}_{3}$ and ${b}_{1}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb1c773-5efa-5640-802f-7172570b982c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317665} {:answer/id #uuid "37e6251c-c663-504a-9ae0-4e99abf04d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317744} {:answer/id #uuid "63d7c35a-f384-50a4-9462-b80fd457652e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317906} {:answer/id #uuid "d39ad45e-1f94-51d7-a97b-6fce87394432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318268} {:answer/id #uuid "da8784b4-eb6d-52d4-a21f-b740a1c6ea6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d39ad45e-1f94-51d7-a97b-6fce87394432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318268} :answer-field/id #uuid "7745095e-0993-59ff-9359-1232381545d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b1d1e88-f02a-5243-9bdc-9d244b4db578" :question/math-academy-id "q-34902" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.$\n- If $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.$\n- If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285ed021-2a5b-563c-9cb6-a55ef00e5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691} {:answer/id #uuid "7e27ee27-ef83-527f-ad6e-6ed1940373d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317995} {:answer/id #uuid "882c9335-98b9-51dd-8014-126aafeeb87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318026} {:answer/id #uuid "982ad340-ed13-552d-bca5-2129fbb6d423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318085} {:answer/id #uuid "f19ae1c4-9c02-5103-bc45-ec9e3b3e8123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285ed021-2a5b-563c-9cb6-a55ef00e5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691} :answer-field/id #uuid "49038ece-c8a5-5724-aab1-227760f5017a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56ebc46d-299b-5fda-a402-a93bacc04806" :question/math-academy-id "q-34973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n- If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=3.$\n- If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5773458f-df7a-5245-86e7-3a92a69e88d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319650} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8164e5-820d-5513-92b6-8f3287537d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319662} {:answer/id #uuid "8209e272-3342-5cbf-a42d-452544386fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319785} {:answer/id #uuid "a054d8d6-0a2e-571c-9e5b-abfed49f29a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319870} {:answer/id #uuid "cd414b52-f822-597e-9d56-a8ec8708dc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd414b52-f822-597e-9d56-a8ec8708dc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320018} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e2a7aba-d31b-5779-b9a2-2f6f5404a48f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3cfa016-2023-56d8-80a8-ced31694c0b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35263" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+({x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(3{c}_{2})+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}_{1}){c}_{2}+{x}_{2}{c}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${3{c}_{2},{c}_{1}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{c}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}.$ This means that ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293ae16a-3b0c-55c8-8fd1-0b62cdbc95fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319523} {:answer/id #uuid "9746878c-7c14-5d13-a1ef-36f1e770e71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319840} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fa24c4-cd40-5277-9097-8340a67b9342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319876} {:answer/id #uuid "bf31823e-18bc-580e-b915-028a69bb1c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319977} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6a7b23-9a34-5d28-9f7b-301a3b7c8c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce6a7b23-9a34-5d28-9f7b-301a3b7c8c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320024} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e7ddf0-1cef-5068-bb9a-a3a4cbecd4b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43599508-ddd2-50d6-b640-692e29bcfb0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-35283" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $6?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $6$ must have at least $6$ columns and at least $6$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $A$ since it has only $5$ columns and the matrix $B$ since it has only $4$ columns.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $6$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $C$ has exactly $6$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(C)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c78bfa2-14dd-582b-852b-ca7700249537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321491} {:answer/id #uuid "301e783a-5111-5681-8b24-c0b31cc6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321516} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d62ed0-9790-5c2d-af76-f2b03f3b7fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322472} {:answer/id #uuid "cb454d8c-6d6c-5aa5-8bc0-5dd114da5a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322727} {:answer/id #uuid "d879594f-5354-5863-8eee-b752be0672b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d62ed0-9790-5c2d-af76-f2b03f3b7fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322472} :answer-field/id #uuid "814d813a-dbf0-5b90-a6cd-f593a61a455e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9288acd6-ddf2-55d8-ba92-e1c27cfdfe7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35425" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$  is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733d342d-f493-5b3b-9805-475f6b936877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322047} {:answer/id #uuid "986be932-03c2-56b6-bd0c-2ab9da015c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322358} {:answer/id #uuid "c376d580-ea6c-5732-9ac7-8d834ce18e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322663} {:answer/id #uuid "dd094e01-d98b-5586-8bcb-8acb73c66058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322876} {:answer/id #uuid "fbacb8bf-917b-5727-9c7c-3a7008487f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733d342d-f493-5b3b-9805-475f6b936877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322047} :answer-field/id #uuid "293acd8f-b072-55a8-83fe-3b797e5c0f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d143826-9093-5bc1-bb87-8034a4c54f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-35599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2}$\n- $C={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{2}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩.$ So, $A={0}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x=\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ since $0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+2.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e5982d-8cad-5451-a451-851b3a2b0a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321676} {:answer/id #uuid "8632c9a2-1c74-5336-a18d-0b2321919992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322212} {:answer/id #uuid "86cc92b0-9666-5afb-8b02-63d47d20dd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322217} {:answer/id #uuid "a74f073c-21a4-586c-b5c1-fefb81f6d49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322456} {:answer/id #uuid "f23caa21-e566-59c8-9ced-a295656d2cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e5982d-8cad-5451-a451-851b3a2b0a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321676} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4200492-e359-5f57-ad7f-72c9043e3672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2aec92e-c3f4-5cb5-bae0-799d4d7e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-35600" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec79ffa15b3ed25d8f439f575e1cdffc3122ae1d82e1b55500a6e5e2e17cf4fc.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace.  Every subspace must contain the origin,  but from the diagram, we can easily see that the origin is not in the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$  without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a7cee8-9323-5fac-be9d-6ea14e91660d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321339} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd18fc9-e6a6-5db0-a4ad-bd939f6fe841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321650} {:answer/id #uuid "60a3aa1b-2af0-5937-b7dd-ea22200db6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321905} {:answer/id #uuid "b66c33e0-216f-5120-bdf0-03ebaaabec1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322566} {:answer/id #uuid "dafcb41a-22c5-5c3b-bbcc-5918ef1969a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b66c33e0-216f-5120-bdf0-03ebaaabec1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322566} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb275cd-5bbf-5cf9-9048-108f7625e28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18aef812-0fac-5da2-8d8b-c2702b3daa12" :question/math-academy-id "q-35663" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)\\in S,$ where $f(x)=2x.$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$ However, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2x\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $S$ such that their sum is *not* in $S.$ For example, let $p(x)=x+{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=x-{x}^{2}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $S$ since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))=2\\ge 2.$ However,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x)+q(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡((x+{x}^{2})+(x-{x}^{2}))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1≱2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0012fd2e-385d-5cc9-b561-834a8efc894c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321148} {:answer/id #uuid "1b339094-1eff-5cd1-96a6-4dac199fd3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321356} {:answer/id #uuid "66a90d44-c600-5cfa-881f-bd6e115ed0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321948} {:answer/id #uuid "e7415cfa-e612-55f4-a49e-6e8d202d44ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322959} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f08233-850d-5156-b1e8-5cedc27b726b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1f08233-850d-5156-b1e8-5cedc27b726b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323048} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9caeef-2a9c-553a-b333-82f7cb3f9e71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c118a776-ac53-56cc-b9e8-1173bce1a8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-35754" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59011896-39e7-52bf-8261-a97745a7ea6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321848} {:answer/id #uuid "9083ca4f-1840-5d1f-bbd0-a3aebcfb6ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322293} {:answer/id #uuid "947fb86d-b16d-5151-bc45-bfff1a5ca857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322327} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d93329-76ba-546c-ae25-7c267f4496a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322815} {:answer/id #uuid "e83b1617-67a3-5666-a040-d549cf9c83ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9083ca4f-1840-5d1f-bbd0-a3aebcfb6ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322293} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08cc063-fbcd-53b1-b1a1-f8bf94126e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69ab5c8f-74d2-578a-ab14-a3f834908b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36002" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c51c5a4de562e5a3301ea76afc6af57b459acd4c46e5c65d54afc226d883a137.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,0]$\n- $x\\in (-3,1)$\n- $x\\in [0,3)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3).$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/00/00a80dde24ca012e0282362224c8b63764f5e149a8bfcda9c76ff4eb958fa93a.png)\n\n \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea54918e2ef4310b58e9cf8a0c9b1f9757a404d97dbbfd9e5b037af2193f38c.png)\n\n \n\nHence, the correct answer is 'I and III only''."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031} :answer-field/id #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in [-6,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [-6,4)$\n\nIII. $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4a999d-ad52-54bc-9609-1a635cfc7e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321250} {:answer/id #uuid "348fd450-639f-5e56-82a6-c291897db931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321546} {:answer/id #uuid "39986b82-2ab5-5a5f-86db-e904841e01c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321585} {:answer/id #uuid "755404f4-77ec-54b2-be4e-0c0e2010bde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322061} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f1bbb5-62b5-5d66-98b9-4e12a9d81779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348fd450-639f-5e56-82a6-c291897db931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321546} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2fd798-c90d-5e86-9792-5a4f0ef3841a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f06fc453-0309-58aa-ad2f-fecdd1ab38c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36008" :question/problem "![](images/27/279c3cd6084423d913186f4ba467ea50d0f794cf54773785777cb8841f5c2ca1.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-1,3]$\n- $x\\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-1]$ and on $x\\in [0,1].$  Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/03/0388335095ed530ce07d278f82be5eb3bff14859db64b6e9ec47da27861bb657.png)\n\n    \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval. $x\\in [-1,3]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/52/522b5661aaf9b7640c305b8cefd06f49cc8eed590f3ae736fc6720e6165bc3a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa590220-f3a0-5655-826b-3efab531bea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322487} {:answer/id #uuid "d0287869-916d-5063-b126-5876daa4c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322772} {:answer/id #uuid "df32af73-fc23-5cf6-aef3-aef4290f85f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322893} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc19260-952f-5ed7-b689-d9003b659719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323014} {:answer/id #uuid "edcdd0a3-12d3-570f-bd72-a7840745eedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0287869-916d-5063-b126-5876daa4c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322772} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd1f6e7-a3d9-5ab4-bf8e-1a83738037a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aef31e6b-2918-5842-b5c4-348781827064" :question/math-academy-id "q-36010" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-2|$ by $1$ unit up to get $f(x)=|x-2|+1.$\n\n![](images/ce/ceab1637313ae53869cc1e59f6a56ad6cb920a83dffba482558b696188df0d36.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (2,4].$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a4/a47169838785b6f4ac78253252bcedc3c2cb752a655feac9945e20dd40563b4f.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/14/14ed35294e1179ffd9efaf87fa8ac1eb99853a7da344e462c2cb0dda74c3b78f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321452} {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} {:answer/id #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" :question/math-academy-id "q-36011" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\nI. $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n\nII. $x\\in [-2,3]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a752cd03-e1ff-5c32-8f10-178e7e9218d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322457} {:answer/id #uuid "a771b7ab-43c5-5525-a9fb-05bbc7302e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322458} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1ceebc-74c0-5218-9e7d-3159c966c78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322593} {:answer/id #uuid "f57853c9-16ac-5a37-9f8c-cd5af765e3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323071} {:answer/id #uuid "ff734961-d876-5369-b9de-ce143aeaddb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a771b7ab-43c5-5525-a9fb-05bbc7302e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322458} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef8d361-c921-5422-b2d5-2716ac5f2af0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0429905-029f-5585-ac21-8d23fa4141fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-36063" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1dd593e8b48b13ec512a3e0c823ec0aaac4f5341bd519c7bb9b04648f95f4251.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/2a/2a68cbeceea9173ab7a53d334d073e9779b048ad8946fd3ff81b09adb79cee98.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ede2f9-bcb4-564f-a5fc-a28d1e65b9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321552} {:answer/id #uuid "52f7ae88-6bed-5e9e-8c6b-6cc3cca014d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321801} {:answer/id #uuid "62d24ea0-f0bb-5ac7-acd6-d7c6a113ce1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321918} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3f310a-5ce6-582f-90a1-56d84b1b111c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322008} {:answer/id #uuid "770ff454-1196-5009-93b5-e58b99517c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34ede2f9-bcb4-564f-a5fc-a28d1e65b9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321552} :answer-field/id #uuid "889396dc-e947-54a7-be54-7f27632807e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6815ae09-6f05-5b7b-9ca9-f709c3e26769" :question/math-academy-id "q-36066" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2065775ae6372e8c820b5e2f19f6f105e032dcd37db1e32e04af5bfa6a4faf9.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$\n- $x\\in (0,2]$\n- $x\\in [1,4)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-2]\\cup (2,4)$ and $x\\in (0,2].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/3c/3c5bc724ee29aee7bef2790012bf0e73ce1ed087134213dbad36a670fd136a36.png)\n\n        \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[1,4)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/03/03b9d70513a4ef99b39c9f05d0c0ac803f8e5611d0967015e9c1d9c0f45bd418.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d0294c-efb3-587e-905a-3c1b8e2615e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321826} {:answer/id #uuid "65f0fcbf-a291-5708-a596-bef3bcdab317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321937} {:answer/id #uuid "d6939f50-a62f-5c42-ba4a-3fc659ffa0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322823} {:answer/id #uuid "e92f675e-e3b4-5bf3-a460-64ebffc64401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322975} {:answer/id #uuid "ec305288-e455-5047-aff1-0f1c99fd0dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6939f50-a62f-5c42-ba4a-3fc659ffa0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322823} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3a4bf3-4101-5c9a-b2b3-613ebd7f6042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b252a44d-8417-5bdc-8a9b-ad6e81276b7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36075" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4609ea70-2120-59c0-878b-4cf3c1315134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321700} {:answer/id #uuid "55321c64-88d5-5d5b-88af-6323a15eb03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321820} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f7408-e5f6-563f-be24-0da0635c6d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322112} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e9caa1-2e91-532c-9454-1a1145f64bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322533} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c61b03-6d81-5a5b-8a13-32ebee29f916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f7408-e5f6-563f-be24-0da0635c6d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322112} :answer-field/id #uuid "655bfab7-299d-5ede-bce9-83e70cf3dc8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f51862bd-578b-533f-86e5-e297bb989e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36244" :question/problem "To which of the following sets do all the real numbers belong?\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$\n- $N$\n- $Q$\n- ${Q}^{-}$" :question/worked-solution "- The set ${Z}^{-}$ contains all the negative integer numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set $N$ contains all the natural numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to $N,$ so $N$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set $Q$ contains all the rational numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to $Q,$ so $Q$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set ${Q}^{-}$ contains all the negative rational numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to ${Q}^{-},$ so ${Q}^{-}$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}]
